command\' module. Then, the \'args\' can contain the command as a list.\n\nWait, no. Let me think again. The \'executable\' parameter in the \'command\' module is separate from the \'args\'. So the correct way is to set \'executable: no\' and then the \'argsexecutable\' would be the full path. Alternatively, perhaps the \'executable\' parameter is not needed if we\'re specifying the full path in \'argsexecutable\'.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct approach is to set \'executable: no\' and then have \'argsexecutable\' as \'/usr/bin/mysql_install_db\', but I\'m not sure if that\'s the correct key. Alternatively, perhaps the \'executable\' parameter is not needed, and the \'args\' should just contain the command as a list.\n\nWait, perhaps the \'executable\' parameter is not necessary here. Let me look at the Ansible \'command\' module documentation. The \'executable\' parameter is a boolean that, if set to \'yes\', makes Ansible not prepend the executable with the full path, but if \'no\', it does. Wait, no, actually, the \'executable\' parameter is to specify whether to use the command as an executable from the PATH or to use the full path. So if you provide the full path in \'executable\', it\'s treated as the exact executable to use.\n\nWait, no, the \'executable\' parameter is a boolean. So if you set it to \'yes\', the command is executed as is, from the PATH. If \'no\', it\'s executed with the full path. So if you want to execute \'/usr/bin/mysql_install_db\', you should set \'executable: no\' and the command would be \'mysql_install_db\'.\n\nWait, no, perhaps the \'executable\' parameter is not needed if you\'re providing the full path in \'argsexecutable\'. Alternatively, perhaps the \'executable\' parameter is a boolean, and the \'argsexecutable\' is a separate parameter. But in the original playbook, they have \'executable: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\', which is incorrect because \'executable\' expects a boolean.\n\nSo to fix this, I should change \'executable: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\' to \'executable: no\' and then set the \'argsexecutable\' to \'/usr/bin/mysql_install_db\'. But wait, the \'args\' parameter is a list of arguments, not the executable. So perhaps the correct approach is to remove the \'executable\' parameter from \'args\' and instead set \'executable: no\' in the \'command\' module.\n\nWait, perhaps the \'executable\' parameter in the \'args\' is incorrect. Let me check the YAML structure. The \'args\' are defined as:\n\nargs:\n    executable: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\n    chdir: /var/lib/mysql\n\nBut \'executable\' is a boolean, so this is wrong. Instead, the \'executable\' should be set in the main \'command\' module parameters, not in \'args\'. So the correct structure would be:\n\nname: Initialize mysql\ncommand: mysql_install_db\nargs:\n    chdir: /var/lib/mysql\nexecutable: no\n\nWait, no, the \'executable\' is a parameter of the \'command\' module, not of the \'args\'. So the correct way is:\n\n- name: Initialize mysql\n  command: mysql_install_db\n  args:\n    chdir: /var/lib/mysql\n  executable: no\n\nBut in the original playbook, they have \'executable\' under \'args\', which is incorrect. So to fix this, I need to move \'executable\' to the main \'command\' parameters.\n\nBut wait, in the original playbook, the \'executable\' is under \'args\', which is wrong. So I need to correct that by moving it to the main \'command\' parameters.\n\nSo the corrected Task 5 should be:\n\n- name: Initialize mysql\n  command: mysql_install_db\n  args:\n    chdir: /var/lib/mysql\n  executable: no\n  vars:\n    become_user: mysql\n\nThis way, \'executable\' is correctly set to \'no\', indicating that the full path is provided, but wait, no, because \'executable\' is a boolean, and the actual executable is specified in \'command\'. So perhaps the \'executable\' parameter isn\'t needed here because \'command\' is the executable to run. Alternatively, if \'command\' is \'mysql_install_db\', and we need to run it with the full path, we should set \'executable: no\'.\n\nWait, perhaps the \'executable\' parameter is redundant here because the \'command\' module is already given the executable as \'mysql_install_db\', which is in the PATH. So if we want to run it with the full path, we should set \'executable: no\', but the command would still be \'mysql_install_db\'. Alternatively, if we set \'executable: yes\', it would use the command as is.\n\nWait, I\'m getting a bit confused. Let me check the Ansible documentation for the \'command\' module. According to the docs, the \'executable\' parameter is a boolean. If set to \'yes\', the command is executed as given, without prepending the executable. If \'no\', the executable is looked up in the PATH. Wait, no, that\'s the opposite. Actually, if \'executable\' is \'yes\', the command is treated as an executable and run directly, without looking up the PATH. If \'no\', it\'s looked up in the PATH.\n\nWait, no, that\'s not correct. Let me clarify: the \'executable\' parameter determines whether the command is treated as an executable (without prepending PATH) or whether it\'s looked up in the PATH. So if you set \'executable: yes\', the command is executed as is, without checking the PATH. If \'executable: no\', the command is looked up in the PATH, and then executed with the full path.\n\nSo in the original playbook, the \'executable\' under \'args\' is set to \'/usr/bin/mysql_install_db\', which is a string, but it should be a boolean. So to fix this, I need to change that to \'executable: no\' and adjust the \'command\' accordingly.\n\nWait, no, perhaps the \'executable\' parameter isn\'t needed if we\'re providing the full path in the \'command\' parameter. So the \'command\' module can be called with the full path directly.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion comes from how the \'executable\' and \'command\' parameters work together. The \'command\' module runs a command on the remote system. The \'executable\' parameter controls whether the command is treated as an executable (without PATH) or looked up in the PATH.\n\nSo, if I want to run \'/usr/bin/mysql_install_db\', I can set \'executable: yes\' and \'command: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\', or I can set \'executable: no\' and \'command: mysql_install_db\'.\n\nIn the original playbook, the \'executable\' is set to the full path, which is incorrect. So to fix it, I should set \'executable: yes\' and \'command: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\', or set \'executable: no\' and \'command: mysql_install_db\'.\n\nBut in the original playbook, the \'command\' is \'mysql_install_db\', and \'executable\' is set to the full path. So perhaps the correct approach is to remove the \'executable\' from \'args\' and set it in the main parameters.\n\nWait, perhaps the \'executable\' parameter is not needed if we\'re using the full path in \'command\'. So, in the \'command\' module, if \'executable: yes\', the command is run as is, without checking PATH. So I can set \'executable: yes\' and \'command: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\'.\n\nAlternatively, if I set \'executable: no\', the command is looked up in PATH, so I just need to set \'command: mysql_install_db\'.\n\nIn the original playbook, they have \'executable\' as a string, which is wrong. So to fix it, I can either:\n\n1. Set \'executable: yes\' and \'command: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\', or\n2. Set \'executable: no\' and \'command: mysql_install_db\'.\n\nI think the first option is better because it explicitly uses the full path, avoiding any dependency on the PATH variable on the remote system.\n\nSo, the corrected Task 5 would be:\n\n- name: Initialize mysql\n  command: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\n  args:\n    chdir: /var/lib/mysql\n  executable: yes\n  vars:\n    become_user: mysql\n\nBut wait, in the original playbook, \'executable\' was under \'args\', which is incorrect. So moving it to the main parameters is necessary.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the \'executable\' parameter isn\'t needed because the \'command\' is already the full path. So setting \'executable: yes\' would make it use the command as is.\n\nWait, perhaps the \'executable\' parameter is redundant when the command is the full path. Let me check: if I set \'executable: yes\' and \'command: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\', it will execute that command directly. If I set \'executable: no\' and \'command: mysql_install_db\', it will look up the executable in the PATH.\n\nIn this case, since the original playbook intended to run the installation with the full path, setting \'executable: yes\' and \'command: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\' would be appropriate.\n\nSo, to fix the errors:\n\n1. Remove the trailing spaces on line 10 (Task 1).\n2. Correct the \'executable\' parameter in Task 5 by moving it to the main \'command\' parameters and setting it to \'yes\' or \'no\' appropriately.\n3. Do the same for the other instance of \'executable\' in Task 5, which is also under \'args\' and causing the same error.\n\nWait, looking back, in the original playbook, Task 5 has both \'executable\' and \'argsexecutable\' set incorrectly. So both need to be fixed.\n\nSo, the corrected Task 5 should have \'executable\' as a boolean in the main parameters, and \'args\' should only contain \'chdir\'.\n\nSo, the corrected Task 5 would be:\n\n- name: Initialize mysql\n  command: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\n  args:\n    chdir: /var/lib/mysql\n  executable: yes\n  vars:\n    become_user: mysql\n\nAlternatively, if we don\'t need \'executable: yes\' because the command is the full path, perhaps it\'s not needed. But to be explicit, setting \'executable: yes\' makes it clear.\n\nAdditionally, the same issue occurs in Task 5 again when \'executable\' is set under \'args\' for the second time. Wait, no, in the original playbook, Task 5 only has one \'executable\' under \'args\'. Wait, looking back, the original playbook has:\n\n- name: Initialize mysql\n  command: mysql_install_db\n  args:\n    executable: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\n    chdir: /var/lib/mysql\n  vars:\n    become_user: mysql\n    executable: mysql_install_db\n\nWait, that\'s a bit confusing. So the \'executable\' is under \'args\' as a string, which is wrong. And the \'executable\' is also set in \'vars\' as \'mysql_install_db\', which is also incorrect because \'executable\' is a parameter of the \'command\' module, not a variable.\n\nSo, to fix Task 5:\n\n- Remove the \'executable\' from \'args\' and \'vars\'.\n- Set \'executable: yes\' in the main parameters of the \'command\' module.\n- Set \'command: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\'.\n\nSo the corrected Task 5 would be:\n\n- name: Initialize mysql\n  command: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\n  args:\n    chdir: /var/lib/mysql\n  executable: yes\n  vars:\n    become_user: mysql\n\nThis way, \'executable\' is correctly set as a boolean, and the command runs with the full path.\n\nNow, for the other error on line 60:23, which is the same issue. Looking at line 60, it\'s the \'executable\' parameter in the \'command\' task again. Wait, no, line 60 is the \'executable\' in the \'mysql_user\' loop. Wait, let me count the lines.\n\nWait, the original playbook has 13 tasks. The \'error messages\' point to line 26 and 60. Let me recount the lines in the original playbook.\n\nWait, the original playbook starts with --- and then the tasks. Let\'s count:\n\n1. Task 1: line 1-3\n2. Task 2: line 4-7\n3. Task 3: line 8-11\n4. Task 4: line 12-14\n5. Task 5: line 15-20\n6. Task 6: line 21-23\n7. Task 7: line 24-25\n8. Task 8: line 26-29\n9. Task 9: line 30-32\n10. Task 10: line 33-34\n11. Task 11: line 35-37\n12. Task 12: line 38-42\n13. Task 13: line 43-45\n\nWait, that doesn\'t add up. Let me actually look at the original playbook.\n\nThe original playbook has:\n\n- name: Import assert.yml\n  ansible.builtin.include_tasks:\n    file: assert.yml\n\n- name: Install mysql\n  apt:\n    name: "{{ packages }}"\n    state: present\n  notify: \n    - mysql_root_password\n    - daemon_reload\n\n- name: Configure mysql server\n  template:\n    src: my.cnf.j2\n    dest: /etc/mysql/my.cnf\n    mode: 0o0644\n    validate_mode: strict\n  notify: mysql_server_restart\n\n- name: Configure mysql client\n  ansible.builtin.lineinfile:\n    path: /etc/mysql/my.cnf\n    line: "{{ client_socket }}"\n    create: no\n\n- name: Initialize mysql\n  command: mysql_install_db\n  args:\n    executable: /usr/bin/mysql_install_db\n    chdir: /var/lib/mysql\n  vars:\n    become_user: mysql\n    executable: mysql_install_db\n\n- name: Start and enable mysql\n  ansible.builtin.systemd:\n    name: "{{ mysql_service_name }}"\n    state: started\n    enabled: true\n\n- name: Flush handlers\n  ansible.builtin.flush_handlers:\n    name: flush_handlers\n\n- name: Place my.cnf\n  template:\n    src: my.cnf.j2\n    dest: /etc/mysql/my.cnf\n    mode: 0o0644\n    validate_mode: strict\n  notify: mysql_server_restart\n\n- name: Create databases\n  mysql_db:\n    name: "{{ item }}"\n    state: present\n    priv: all:all\n    flush_privileges: yes\n  loop: "{{ mysql_databases }}"\n\n- name: List databases\n  mysql_db:\n    name: "*"\n    state: list\n\n- name: Import database\n  mysql_db:\n    name: "{{ item }}"\n    state: import\n    initial_import: "{{ initial_import_location }}"\n  loop: "{{ mysql_databases }}"\n  when: initial_import_location is defined\n\n- name: Create users\n  mysql_user:\n    name: "{{ item.name }}"\n    password: "{{ item.password }}"\n    priv: "{{ item.privileges }}"\n    state: present\n  loop: "{{ mysql_users }}"\n  notify: mysql_server_restart\n\n- name: Flush handlers again\n  ansible.builtin.flush_handlers:\n    name: flush_handlers\n\nSo, line numbers:\n\n- Task 5 starts at line 15, and the \'executable\' is on line 18 (args: executable: ...), which is line 18. But the error message says line 26:13. Hmm, perhaps I\'m miscounting.\n\nWait, perhaps the line numbers in the error messages are from the entire playbook, not per-task. Let me check:\n\nLooking at the original playbook, line 10 would be the end of Task 1:\n\n- name: Import assert.yml\n  ansible.builtin.include_tasks:\n    file: assert.yml\n\nThat\'s 3 lines, so line 10 would be the end of Task 1. Then Task 2 starts on line 4 (if we count starting from 1). Wait, perhaps the line numbers are off. Alternatively, perhaps the errors are on lines 10 and 26 in the entire playbook.\n\nBut according to the user\'s message, the errors are:\n\n10:10: trailing spaces (trailing-spaces)\n26:13: truthy value should be one of [false, true] (truthy)\n60:23: truthy value should be one of [false, true] (truthy)\n\nSo, line 10, column 10 has trailing spaces. Looking at the original playbook, line 10 would be the end of Task 1. Let me check:\n\nThe first task is:\n\n- name: Import assert.yml\n  ansible.builtin.include_tasks:\n    file: assert.yml\n\nThis is three lines. If we count each line as starting from 1, line 1 is the first \'-\', line 2 is \'name\', line 3 is \'ansible.builtin...\', and line 4 is \'file: ...\'.\n\nWait, perhaps the line numbers are as follows:\n\nLine 1: --- \nLine 2: \nLine 3: - name: Import assert.yml\nLine 4:   ansible.builtin.include_tasks:\nLine 5:     file: assert.yml\nLine 6: \nLine 7: - name: Install mysql\n...\n\nSo, line 10 would be the end of Task 1, which is line 5. Hmm, perhaps the line numbers are different.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the line numbers are 1-based, so the first line after \'---\' is line 1.\n\nIn any case, the first error is trailing spaces on line 10:10. Looking at the original playbook, the first task ends with \'file: assert.yml\' on line 3 (assuming lines start at 1). Then, there\'s a blank line, so line 4 is the next task.\n\nBut perhaps the error is on line 10, which is in Task 2. Let me look at Task 2:\n\n- name: Install mysql\n  apt:\n    name: "{{ packages }}"\n    state: present\n  notify: \n    - mysql_root_password\n    - daemon_reload\n\nHere, the \'notify\' block has a space after the colon, which is allowed, but perhaps the trailing space is on the next line.\n\nWait, no, the \'notify\' line ends with a colon and then has the list. The error is about trailing spaces, so perhaps the line after \'notify:\' has a space before the \'- mysql_root_password\'.\n\nLooking at the original Task 2, the \'notify\' block is:\n\n  notify: \n    - mysql_root_password\n    - daemon_reload\n\nThe line \'notify: \' has a space after the colon, which is a trailing space. So line 6 (if we count starting from 1) has \'notify: \' with a space, causing a trailing space error.\n\nSo to fix this, we need to remove the space after the colon in the \'notify\' line.\n\nSimilarly, the same issue occurs in other tasks where there\'s a space after the colon in the notify lines.\n\nSo, the first error is in Task 2, line \'notify: \' with a trailing space.\n\nThe second error is in Task 5, where \'executable\' is set to a string, causing a truthy value error.\n\nThe third error is in Task 12, where \'executable\' is again set to a string.\n\nWait, looking back, the user\'s error messages are:\n\n10:10: trailing spaces (trailing-spaces)\n26:13: truthy value should be one of [false, true] (truthy)\n60:23: truthy value should be one of [false, true] (truthy)\n\nSo, line 10 has trailing spaces. Looking at the original playbook, line 10 would be in Task 2\'s \'notify\' line, which has a space after the colon.\n\nSo, the first fix is to remove the trailing space after the colon in the \'notify\' lines.\n\nThe second and third errors are on lines 26 and 60, which are in Task 5 and Task 12, where \'executable\' is set to a string instead of a boolean.\n\nSo, to fix these:\n\n1. Remove trailing spaces in all lines where there\'s a space after the colon.\n2. Correct the \'executable\' parameter in Task 5 and Task 12 to be boolean values (true or false).\n\nWait, but in the original playbook, only Task 5 has \'executable\' under \'args\', and Task 12 has \'executable\' in \'vars\', which is also incorrect.\n\nWait, looking at Task 12 in t