rhaps using the copy module would be more appropriate. That way, I can loop over the list of modules and copy each one.\n\nLet me think about the flow. For each item in the loop, I need to copy a file from mods-available to mods-enabled. So the tasks would be:\n\n- For each module (proxy.load and proxy_http.load), copy them to the mods-enabled directory.\n- Set the correct permissions and ownership.\n\nUsing the copy module would simplify this. So the corrected Task 1 would loop over the list of modules and copy each one. That would eliminate the need for the template module and the associated Jinja2 issues.\n\nSo, I\'ll replace the template module with a copy module. The loop will iterate over the list of modules, and for each, copy the file to the correct destination. I\'ll also set the correct mode, owner, and group as before.\n\nThis approach should fix the Jinja2 error because we\'re no longer using the template module in a way that\'s causing the syntax issue. It also makes the task more straightforward and aligns with Ansible best practices for copying files.\n\nI should also check if there are any other linting issues, but from the error message, it seems the main problem was with the template module\'s src parameter. By replacing it with the copy module, I should resolve the syntax error and make the playbook pass ansible-lint.\n\nAnother thing to consider is whether the files exist in the expected locations. The original task assumes that the modules are in mods-available, which is correct for Debian-based systems. So the copy should work as intended.\n\nIn summary, the fix involves changing the module from template to copy, properly looping over the module files, and copying each one to the mods-enabled directory with the correct permissions. This should eliminate the syntax error and make the playbook valid.\n</think>\n\n---\n- name: locations | Enable modules\n  ansible.builtin.copy:\n    src: "{{ item }}"\n    dest: /etc/apache2/mods-enabled/\n    mode